[Event "41st ECC Open 2026"]
[Site "?"]
[Round "1.1"]
[White "Mamedyarov, S"]
[WhiteElo "2710"]
[Black "Duncan, C"]
[BlackElo "2179"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Annotator "TWIC 1667 – White's Nf3/b3/Bb2 System"]
[Opening "Nf3/b3/Bb2: Isolated d5 pawn model"]

1. c4 e6 2. Nf3 d5 3. g3 a6 4. b3 Nf6 5. Bg2 c5 6. cxd5 exd5 7. O-O Nc6 8. Bb2 Be7 9. d4 O-O
{The central question of every Nf3/b3/Bb2 game is whether White's pressure on the long diagonal will become decisive or whether Black can close it. After 6.cxd5 exd5 Black has a symmetric pawn centre, but after 9.d4 White sets up the ideal formation: the Bb2 points at d4, and once White challenges c5, Black will be left with a lone isolated d5 pawn – the classic weakness in this system.}
10. Nc3 Be6 11. Rc1 Rc8 12. dxc5 Bxc5 13. Na4
{The knight tour to c5 is the main strategic idea. Na4 attacks the bishop and prepares Nc5, which will settle on the blockading square. Once the knight arrives on c5 it dominates the board: it attacks b7 and a6, paralyses Black's queen side, and gives the Bb2 a clear view of d5.}
13... Be7 14. Nc5 Bxc5 15. Rxc5 Qe7 16. Rc1 Rfd8 17. Nd4 Bg4 18. Re1 Qd6 19. h3 Bd7 20. Nxc6 Bxc6
{All the pieces have been exchanged and the position is deceptively quiet. But the structural verdict is in: Black has an isolated d5 pawn on an open file, no light-squared bishop, and a passive bishop on c6 that is merely guarding d5. White's Qd4 battery will pin Black's defensive resources while the kingside attack begins.}
21. Qd4 Qe6 22. Rc3
{The rook lifts. White is in no hurry to attack while Black cannot improve his position. The rook will swing to g3 and then White will push g4-g5-h4-h5. Black must sit and wait.}
22... Re8 23. g4 h6 24. Rg3 Rcd8 25. h4 Re7 26. g5 Ne8 27. e4
{The central break arrives at the perfect moment. The h-file is under threat, Black's pieces are cramped, and the isolated d5 pawn is suddenly exposed to the full force of White's bishop and queen. Mamedyarov opens the game before Black can reorganise.}
27... hxg5 28. hxg5 Qg6 29. f4 Nd6 30. f5 Nxf5 31. exf5 Rxe1+ 32. Kf2 Qxf5+ 33. Kxe1 Qg6 34. Kd2 Bb5 35. Be4 f5 36. Bxd5+ Kf8 37. Ba3+ 1-0

[Event "41st ECC Open 2026"]
[Site "?"]
[Round "3.5"]
[White "Alonso Rosell, A"]
[WhiteElo "2560"]
[Black "Yakubboev, Nodirbek"]
[BlackElo "2712"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Annotator "TWIC 1667 – White's Nf3/b3/Bb2 System"]
[Opening "Nf3/b3/Bb2: Double fianchetto, c-file domination"]

1. Nf3 Nf6 2. g3 b6 3. Bg2 Bb7 4. O-O e6 5. c4 Be7 6. b3 O-O 7. Bb2 Re8 8. Nc3 d5 9. cxd5 exd5 10. e3 Nbd7 11. Re1 a6 12. Rc1 Rc8 13. Re2 Bd6 14. d3 Ne5
{Both sides have developed naturally and the position has the feel of a closed English/QGD hybrid. White's Bb2 points along the long diagonal at d4 – but d4 is just a dream for now, blocked by his own pawn. The plan is to prepare e4 to open the diagonal, but first the rooks are doubled on the c-file to keep Black under constant pressure.}
15. Nxe5 Bxe5 16. Na4
{Rather than straightforward central play, Alonso Rosell launches the knight on its familiar tour to c5. The threat is Nc5 winning the b7 bishop or forcing Ba6, after which the bishop is decentralised.}
16... Bxb2 17. Nxb2 c5
{This is the moment Black has to choose. Taking on b2 gives White the open a-file and a more active knight, but leaving the bishop to be chased away was also awkward. Black plays c5 immediately to challenge in the centre before White consolidates.}
18. Rec2 a5 19. d4 cxd4 20. Qxd4 Rxc2 21. Rxc2 Ba6 22. Na4 Re6 23. Nc3 Rc6 24. h3 Bb7 25. e4
{The e4 break finally comes, opening the long diagonal. The Bg2 now bears down on d5 with full force. Black's bishop is on the wrong colour and the isolated d5 pawn must be defended constantly.}
25... Rc5 26. Rd2 Qc7 27. Rd3 Ba6 28. Re3 dxe4 29. Nxe4 Re5 30. f4 Re7 31. Nxf6+ gxf6
{An important decision. White gives up the knight for structural damage: Black's f6/e-file structure is wrecked and the g8 king is exposed. The Bg2 now dominates from afar.}
32. Rxe7 Qxe7 33. Qxb6 Bd3 34. Qxa5 Qe3+ 35. Kh2 Kg7 36. Qd5 Qe2 37. a4 Qc2 38. Kg1 Qb1+ 39. Kh2 Qc2 40. a5 Bf1 41. b4 Qb2 42. Qf3 Ba6 43. Qc6 Qe2 44. Qe4 Qd2 45. Qc6 Qe2 46. g4 h6 47. Qe4 Qd2 48. Kg3 Qc3+ 49. Bf3 Qc1 50. Qd4 Qe1+ 51. Kh2 Qf1 52. Qe3 Qc4 53. g5 hxg5 54. fxg5 Qc7+ 55. Kg2 Qd6 56. Qc3 Kg8 57. gxf6 Qf4 58. h4 Bc4 59. b5 Bxb5 60. Qc5 Bf1+ 61. Kf2 Qxh4+ 62. Kxf1 Qxf6 63. Qc8+ Kg7 64. Qg4+ Kf8 65. Qb4+ Kg7 66. Kg2 Kg8 67. Qg4+ Kf8 68. Qc8+ Kg7 69. a6 Qb2+ 70. Kh3 Qb6 71. Qc3+ Kg8 72. Qc4 Qh6+ 73. Kg2 Qd2+ 74. Kf1 Qe3 75. Bd5 Kg7 76. Bxf7 1-0

[Event "Atyrau Chess Masters Open"]
[Site "?"]
