[White "Lobanov, Sergei"]
[WhiteElo "2517"]
[Black "Lisenkov, Svyatoslav"]
[BlackElo "2409"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Annotator "TWIC 1667 – White's Nf3/b3/Bb2 System"]
[Opening "Nf3/b3/Bb2: Qa1 battery and c-file invasion"]

1. c4 Nf6 2. g3 c6 3. Nf3 g6 4. b3 Bg7 5. Bb2 d5 6. Bg2 O-O 7. O-O Re8 8. d3 Bg4
{Black seeks counterplay by pinning the knight, a common idea in the King's Indian/Grünfeld hybrid structures. The bishop on g4 can be a nuisance if White doesn't respond accurately.}
9. Nbd2 Nbd7 10. h3 Bxf3 11. Nxf3 e5 12. cxd5 cxd5 13. e3 Bf8 14. Rc1 Bd6 15. Rc2 Qe7 16. Qa1
{The key moment. White's queen retreats all the way to a1 – not a retreat but a strategic redeployment. On a1 it fires along the entire long diagonal at d4 and beyond, while also providing a second support point for the coming c-file operation. The battery of Qa1/Bb2/Bg2 is one of the most characteristic ideas in the whole Nf3/b3/Bb2 system.}
16... Rac8 17. Rfc1 Rxc2 18. Rxc2 Ba3 19. Rc7
{The rook invades the seventh rank, where it will torment Black's queenside. With the queen on a1 covering the long diagonal, Black has no satisfactory response: the bishop on a3 is offside and Black's queen has no good square.}
19... Bxb2 20. Qxb2 Qb4 21. Qc3 Qb6 22. Rc8 Rxc8 23. Qxc8+ Kg7 24. d4
{Now the central break comes, opening the long diagonal at last. The queen on c8 is already causing chaos and d4 will only intensify the pressure.}
24... e4 25. Ne5 Qd6 26. Qh8+ Kxh8 27. Nxf7+
{A queen sacrifice followed by a knight fork – the tactical justification of all White's positional manoeuvres. After 26.Qh8+ Black is forced to take, and 27.Nxf7+ forks king and queen. White wins the queen and the game is effectively over.}
27... Kg7 28. Nxd6 b6 29. Bf1 Kf8 30. Nc8 a5 31. Bb5 h5 32. Kf1 Kf7 33. Ke2 Ke6 34. f4 exf3+ 35. Kxf3 g5 36. h4 gxh4 37. gxh4 Nf8 38. Bd3 b5 39. Kf4 b4 40. Bf5+ Kf7 41. Nd6+ Ke7 42. Nb7 Ne8 43. Nxa5 Kf6 44. Nc6 1-0

[Event "Amager CA Centenary GM"]
