[Black "Szpar, Milosz"]
[BlackElo "2507"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Annotator "TWIC 1667 – White's Nf3/b3/Bb2 System"]
[Opening "Nf3/b3/Bb2: Black's c6/d5 wall, the ...Ne5 antidote"]

1. Nf3 Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. b3 d5 4. Bb2 Bd6 5. g3 O-O 6. Bg2 Nbd7 7. cxd5 exd5
{This game shows the healthy side of Black's position. By keeping the pawn on d5 instead of recapturing with the e-pawn, Black maintains a solid pawn chain c6-d5 – a wall that the Bb2 cannot easily chip away at. The bishop bites on granite.}
8. O-O Re8 9. Nc3 c6 10. Re1 Ne5
{The key antidote. Black's knight goes to e5, offering to trade itself for White's most active piece, the Nf3. This trade relieves the pressure on d5 (the knight was helping White aim at that square) and leaves Black with a very solid structure.}
11. Nxe5 Bxe5 12. d4 Bd6
{After the trade, Black retreats the bishop to d6 where it remains a solid defender of the d5-e6 pawn chain. White has gained space with d4 but Black's structure is rock solid: d5/c6 is immovable and Black will seek counterplay with ...Ne4 or ...h5.}
13. Qd3 Be6 14. Rad1 h5
{An ambitious kingside thrust. Black does not wait for White's queenside initiative but immediately creates tension on the kingside. The idea is ...h4 to open lines, or simply to make Rh3 tricks harder for White.}
15. h4 Bg4 16. Bc1 Qd7 17. Bg5 Nh7 18. Bd2 Re7 19. Rc1 Rd8 20. e4
{White finally strikes in the centre, but Black is ready.}
20... Ba3
{A beautiful deflecting move. The bishop attacks c1, forcing the rook to move, and after the rook moves the d5 advance is no longer a problem. Black's piece coordination is excellent.}
21. exd5 Bxc1 22. Rxc1 Ree8 23. dxc6 bxc6 24. Be3 Nf6 25. Qa6 Rc8 26. Qa5 Bf5 27. Bf3 Ng4 28. Bxg4 Bxg4 29. Kh2 c5 30. Qb5 Qf5 31. Qf1 Rxe3 32. fxe3 Qxf1 33. Rxf1 cxd4 34. Nd5 Rc2+ 35. Kg1 d3 36. Nb4 Rc3 37. Nd5 Rc5 38. Nf4 d2 39. Nd3 Be2 0-1

