[Annotator "TWIC 1667 – White's Nf3/b3/Bb2 System"]
[Opening "Nf3/b3/Bb2: Aggressive g4-g5 storm and queenside castling"]

1. Nf3 d5 2. e3 Nf6 3. c4 dxc4 4. Na3 c5 5. Nxc4 e6 6. b3 Be7 7. Bb2 O-O 8. g4
{A provocative and aggressive choice. Instead of the usual g3 fianchetto, Sjugirov pushes g4 immediately, announcing that this game will be decided by a direct kingside attack. The Bb2 is not yet developed but White prioritises attack over elegance. If the g-pawn reaches g5 it will dislodge Black's knight and expose the king.}
8... Nc6 9. g5 Ne4 10. Rg1 Nd6 11. Nxd6 Qxd6 12. Qc2 Rd8 13. Bb5 Qd5 14. Qc3
{White's queen slides to c3, forming a battery with the Bb2 on the long diagonal that points at g7. At the same time, the queen controls e5, and White is ready to castle queenside and then roll the h-pawn forward with devastating effect.}
14... Bf8 15. Bxc6 bxc6 16. Ne5 a5 17. Ng4 Ra7 18. O-O-O
{Castles long – the signal that White is going all-in on the kingside. The king is safe behind the queenside pawns and the rooks will connect along the g and h files. Black's king on g8 is the target.}
18... Kh8 19. h4 a4 20. h5 axb3 21. axb3 e5 22. Nxe5 Kg8 23. g6 f6 24. gxh7+ Kxh7 25. Qc2+
{The queen joins the attack with check. Black's king has nowhere to hide and the h-file will decide.}
25... Kg8 26. Ng4 Bxg4 27. Rxg4 Qxh5 28. Qc4+ Qd5 29. Bxf6 Rb8 30. e4 Qxc4+ 31. bxc4 Rb4 32. d3 Ra2 33. Rdg1 Rxf2 34. Bxg7 Bxg7 35. Rxg7+ Kf8 36. Rg8+ Ke7 37. R1g7+ Kd6 38. Rd8+ Ke5 39. Re7+ Kf6 40. Rf8+ Kxe7 41. Rf7+ 1-0

