[Annotator "TWIC 1667 – White's Nf3/b3/Bb2 System"]
[Opening "Zukertort: Ne5, f4, Rf3-h3, g4 kingside attack"]

1. d4 Nf6 2. Nf3 d5 3. e3 e6 4. Bd3 c5 5. b3 Nc6 6. Bb2 b6 7. Nbd2 Bb7 8. O-O Rc8 9. Qe2 cxd4 10. exd4 Bd6 11. a3 O-O 12. Ne5
{The Zukertort move order gives White a subtly different structure: here the bishop is on d3 rather than g2, aiming directly at h7. After 12.Ne5 White occupies the key e5 outpost, controls the board's centre of gravity, and prepares the f4 advance. This is the most direct attacking approach in the whole Bb2 family.}
12... Ne7 13. f4 Ng6 14. Rf3
{The rook swings to f3, ready to transfer to h3 or g3. This is the signature move of the Zukertort attack: instead of slowly building pressure, White takes the rook off the back rank and launches a direct assault on the kingside. The threat of Rh3 or Rxf6 is permanent.}
14... Qc7 15. Raf1 Ne4 16. Rh3
{The rook arrives on h3 with devastating effect. The threat is Rxh7 or Nxf7 and White's pieces are all pointing at the kingside. Black cannot defuse the attack by normal means.}
16... f5 17. Nxe4 dxe4 18. Bc4 Rf6 19. g4
{The pawn storm begins. After 19...fxg4 20.Qxg4 White opens the g-file and the attack is unstoppable. Black's kingside structure is demolished.}
19... fxg4 20. Qxg4 Bd5 21. Bxd5 exd5 22. Rc3 Bxe5 23. Qxc8+
{A brilliant queen sacrifice that wins decisively. After 23...Rxc8 24.Rxc7 Rxc7 White is up an exchange and the rook dominates the seventh rank. Alternatively if 23...Nf8 Qxf8+ Rxf8 24.Rxc7 wins the queen with interest. The game concludes swiftly.}
1-0

