[Annotator "TWIC 1667 – White's Nf3/b3/Bb2 System"]
[Opening "Nf3/b3/Bb2: a4-a5 queenside clamp, d5 break opens diagonal"]

1. Nf3 d5 2. e3 Nf6 3. c4 e6 4. Nc3 Bd6 5. b3 O-O 6. Bb2 Re8 7. d4 b6 8. Bd3 Bb7 9. O-O Nbd7 10. a4
{White begins queenside expansion immediately. The plan of a4-a5 is a fundamental idea in this structure: by pushing the a-pawn, White clamps down on b6 and makes Black's queenside pawns rigid. Once a5 lands, the b6 pawn becomes a permanent target and Black can never generate queenside counterplay.}
10... a6 11. a5
{This move completely changes the character of the position. Black's b6 pawn is now fixed on a light square and cannot advance. The queenside is locked in White's favour, which means the game will be decided by play in the centre and on the kingside.}
11... dxc4 12. bxc4 e5
{Black opens the centre to generate counterplay before White consolidates. The bishop on b2 is about to become very active.}
13. Bf5 exd4 14. exd4 Nf8 15. d5
{The thematic break. White pushes d5, closing the centre to stop Black's pieces from becoming active and simultaneously opening the long diagonal: the Bb2 now has a clear path from b2 through d4 all the way to the kingside. This is the moment the entire game has been building towards.}
15... Ng6 16. Qc2 Ne5 17. Nxe5 Bxe5 18. Rfe1 Qd6 19. g3 b5 20. Ne4 Nxe4 21. Qxe4 Bxb2
{Black forces the exchange of the Bb2, correctly identifying it as the key long-range attacker. But it is too late.}
22. Qxe8+ Qf8 23. Qxf8+ Kxf8
{An exchange of queens leads to an endgame where White's passed d5 pawn, supported by the rooks and the Bd3, is a decisive force.}
24. Ra2 Bc3 25. Re3 b4 26. Rae2 g6 27. Bd7 Kg7 28. Ba4 Bc8 29. Re8 1-0
